# Chemical Topology and Complexity of Protein Architectures

## abstract
Chemical topology has emerged as one intriguing feature in protein engineering. Nature demonstrates the elegance and power of protein topology engineering in the unique biofunctions and exceptional stabilities of cyclotides and lasso peptides. With entangling protein motifs and genetically encoded peptide-protein chemistry, artificial proteins with complex topologies, including cyclic proteins, star proteins, and protein catenanes, have become accessible. Among them, proteins with mechanical bonds ('mechanoproteins') are of special interest, owing to their potential functional benefits such as structure stabilization, quaternary structure control, synergistic multivalency effect, and dynamic mechanical sliding/switching properties. In this review article, we summarize recent progress in the field of protein topology engineering as well as the challenges and opportunities that it holds.

## Review Chemical Topology and Complexity of Protein Architectures
Chemical topology has emerged as one intriguing feature in protein engineering. Nature demonstrates the elegance and power of protein topology engineering in the unique biofunctions and exceptional stabilities of cyclotides and lasso peptides. With entangling protein motifs and genetically encoded peptide–protein chemistry, artificial proteins with complex topologies, including cyclic proteins, star proteins, and protein catenanes, have become accessible. Among them, proteins with mechanical bonds (‘mechanoproteins’) are of special interest, owing to their potential functional benefits such as structure stabilization, quaternary structure control, synergistic multivalency effect, and dynamic mechanical sliding/switching properties. In this review article, we summarize recent progress in the field of protein topology engineering as well as the challenges and opportunities that it holds.
## Topology as a Unique Dimension of Protein Engineering
Macromolecular chemistry and biology have been tightly related to each other since the early days of polymer science. The study of the molecular origin of genetic information, enzyme catalysis, and immune response has laid the foundation of molecular biology. It is now understood that living matter is based heavily on macromolecular architectures. As such, organisms are also the finest ‘polymer chemists’, who can tackle the grand challenges in making macromolecules with designed precision and function, as demonstrated by the ribosomal synthesis of proteins. However, despite the unparalleled control of sequence, length, and stereochemistry in protein biosynthesis, the diversity of chemical topology of proteins remains largely untapped.
Topology describes the characteristics of an object (molecule) that remain unchanged throughout arbitrary and continuous distortion and deformation without breaking linkages or passing through one another [1]. Topology was introduced into the chemical field by Frisch and Wasserman in 1961 [2] and later stretched to describe the connectivity and spatial neighborhood of atoms, residues, secondary structures, or subunits $(\mathsf{B o x\ 1})$ ). Topology variation often leads to mechanical interlocking, forming so-called ‘mechanical bonds’ $({\mathsf{B o}}\times2)$ . Mechanically interlocked molecules or ‘mechanomolecules’, such as rotaxanes and catenanes, have been actively and intensively pursued since the 1950s and have matured into a flourishing research field [3]. While small mechanomolecules are essential components for molecular machines, synthetic polymers with mechanical bonds have demonstrated equally versatile dynamic properties [4–6]. By contrast, intriguingly, these aspects have been largely understudied in protein engineering [7,8]. Proteins containing mechanical bonds are termed ‘mechanoproteins’ and typically include knotted proteins, lasso peptides, and protein catenanes. The recombinant DNA technology and the recently emerged genetically encodable chemical tools $(\mathsf{B o x3})$ have facilitated the exploration of this unique dimension of proteins via rational manipulation of protein chemical topology [7,9–12]. In fact, chemical topology and mechanostereochemistry
## Highlights
Chemical topology of the protein backbone has become an important dimension in protein engineering for tuning the stability and dynamic properties of proteins.
Natural proteins with nonlinear backbones or nontrivial topologies exist largely in places where exceptional stability is desired.
Mechanoproteins are proteins containing one or more mechanical bonds.
The dynamic nature of mechanical bonds in proteins has been demonstrated in lasso peptides exhibiting thermally switchable properties.
As a powerful toolset, genetically encoded peptide–protein chemistry has facilitated the design and synthesis of artificial proteins with complex topologies including cyclic, branched, tadpole, lasso, rotaxane, and catenane architectures.
