## Box 1. Chemical Topology
Mathematically, topology is a concept to describe the preserved properties of space in continuous deformation. As molecules are 3D, topology is one of their intrinsic properties. This is particularly true for long-chain molecules [13]. The unique symmetry and complexity are also appealing to the broader community of chemists. Synthesis of topologically complex molecules, such as mechanically interlocked molecular ‘catenanes’ was achieved as early as in 1950s. Over the past decades, many tailor-made strategies have been developed to enable the synthesis of a wide spectrum of intricate molecular topologies, including knots, links, and molecular devices [14,15]. During this endeavor, the concept of chemical topology has also been stretched to cover a broader scope of elements concerning connectivity and spatial relationship [1]. Currently, chemical topology describes not only the connectivity of atoms in the molecules but also the spatial arrangement of these structural motifs in 3D space. In small molecules, numerous mechanically interlocked molecules have been developed for use in molecular machines since links are the basis of many devices in mechanical engineering. In macromolecules, topology variation has been routinely practiced to tune their physical properties. In biopolymers such as DNA, complex molecular topologies are common and their interconversions can be mediated by DNA topoisomerases. But only a mere fraction of natural proteins exhibits unconventional protein topologies [16,17].
