## Topological Complexity of Natural Proteins
All ribosomally synthesized nascent proteins possess strictly linear backbones. They subsequently fold into various shapes with occasional entanglements such as knots and undergo post-translational modifications with frequently occurring intramolecular covalent crosslinks such as disulfide bonds. The topological complexity is thus generated. Since disulfide bonds are usually not stable under reducing environments, we mainly consider the topology formed by more stable covalent linkages such as isopeptides and esters in this review. Figure 1 summarizes typical protein topologies. The most common nonlinear proteins are cyclic proteins such as cyclotides [33]. They exist in all kingdoms of life and are often involved in host defense as highly stable chemical weapons [34]. Despite different origins, cyclotides are often genetic products produced from the corresponding linear protein precursors via post-translational processing.
Lasso peptides, the second prevalent nonlinear topology in proteins, are also mostly genetic products obtained after multi-step processing (Figure 2B) and play a broad range of biological roles including receptor antagonism, enzyme inhibition, and antiviral/antimicrobial activities [35]. Lasso peptides feature a macrolactam ring with a tail threading through the ring. The intramolecular entanglement gives rise to a mechanical bond beyond the normal cyclicbranched polymer. The steric hindrance of the bulky side groups forms the ‘plug’ and locks the peptide in the threaded state. Multiple threaded states could exist, as demonstrated in the recently discovered benenodin-1 [18], leading to a thermally actuated [1]rotaxane switch. The involvement of mechanical bonds adds to the complexity of protein topology.
Protein folding involves twisting, entangling, and/or threading of the polypeptide chains. A systematic study revealed that about $6\%$ of all folded protein contain knots [16]. Strictly speaking, these proteins mostly possess open ends and are topologically trivial, not knots in the mathematical sense. Nonetheless, since they are stable in solution, they are still considered as knots in chemical topology and are categorized by assuming a postulated covalent linkage between the N and C termini. King and coworkers introduced algorithms for detecting slip knots [36]. Sulkowska and coworkers developed another algorithm to identify knots from the PDB and summarized their abundances and structural features [17]. Moreover,
## Box 2. Mechanical Bonds, Mechanomolecules, and Mechanoproteins
As proposed by Stoddart and coworkers, mechanical bonds refer to the spatial entanglement between two or more molecular entities (component parts) such that separation would not be possible without breaking chemical bonds [14]. Molecules with one or more mechanical bonds are thus named mechanomolecules, and the study of the relative spatial arrangements of atoms of mechanomolecules is termed mechanostereochemistry. The definition can be extended to proteins. Hence, mechanoproteins could be defined as proteins containing one or more mechanical bonds. In natural proteins, benenodin-1 is a typical mechanoprotein containing a thermally actuated mechanical bond [18], and the capsid of HK97 bacteriophage contains numerous mechanical bonds for exceptional stability [19]. Mechanostereochemistry and chemical topology are two partially overlapping research topics. For example, the Hopf link possesses a mechanical bond between two rings and is topologically distinct from the single-ring counterpart. By contrast, rotaxanes are topologically trivial, yet the steric hindrance and the resulting mechanical bond would make the separation of the two components practically impossible, thus also contributing to the formation of mechanical bonds. The boundary between the two categories is thus somewhat blurred (Figure I).
