## ${\mathsf{B o x}}3$ . Genetically Encoded Peptide–Protein Chemistry (Molecular Superglues)
In nature, intramolecular covalent crosslinks (such as isopeptide bonds) are formed to enhance the protein stability against stress [20]. Inspired by this, Howarth and coworkers developed genetically encoded peptide–protein reactive pairs by splitting such isopeptide-forming domains into a peptide tag and its cognate protein partner [21–23]. The two components reconstitute in situ to restore the autocatalytic activity for spontaneous isopeptide bond formation between them. The covalent linkage is strong and irreversible, leading to a peptide–protein interaction with ‘infinite’ affinity. They are thus named molecular superglues [24,25]. Many different reactive pairs have been developed, among which the SpyTag/SpyCatcher reaction remains the gold standard. Some useful variants include the SpyCatcher double mutant $(\mathsf{B}_{\mathsf{V A}})$ with high reactivity to selective SpyTag variants[26], the supercharged SpyCatcher() [27], and the faster version of SpyTag002/SpyCatcher002 [28]. They can also be developed from other domains. Examples include isopeptag-N/pilin-N (isopeptag-C/pilin-C) from Spy0128 [21], SnoopTag/SnoopCatcher from the adhesin RrgA domain [22], SdyTag/SdyCatcher from Streptococcus dysgalactiae [29], and other pairs from similar CnaB-type domains [30]. It suggests that there is a family of similar peptide– protein reactive pairs with diverse features. Notably, it can be further split into three components where each component contains one of the key residues of the catalytic triad, leading to peptide–peptide ligation mediated by a third component such as SpyLigase [31] or SnoopLigase [32]. All these reactive sequences are completely based on natural amino acids. In addition,thereactionconditionisverymildand fullycompatiblewithcellularenvironments.Therefore, thesereactionscan be programmedinto theprotein sequenceto direct their post-translational modification, control their cellular locations, or make protein nanoassemblies. The possibility to encrypt the information about chemical reactivity into sequence facilitates the diversification of protein chemical topology.
Protein catenanes are a simple but important class of mechanoproteins. Naturally concatenated noncovalent oligomeric rings have been observed in bovine mitochondrial peroxiredoxin III [37], recombination R proteins from Deinococcus radiodurans [38], Escherichia coli class 1a ribonucleotide reductase [39], and $\mathsf{C S}_{2}$ hydrolase in archaeon thermophilic Acidianus A1-3 [40]. Since these interlocked rings of subunits are likely to be disrupted by dissociation of the noncovalent subunit interfaces, they probably would not represent a stabilization mechanism. By contrast, there are only two cases of covalent ring protein catenanes in nature known to date. Citrate synthase, discovered in Pyrobaculum aerophilum, forms a concatenated homodimer where the subunits are connected topologically by internal disulfide bonds [41]. The capsid of bacteriophage HK97 exhibits a strikingly sophisticated and beautiful molecular ‘chain-mail’ structure with hundreds of neighboring interlocked rings, which is obtained after directed assembly and maturation (Figure 2A) [19]. In both cases, thermal stability is dramatically enhanced, showcasing the power of topology engineering and mechanical bonds in protein engineering. To date, there are limited examples of proteins with nontrivial topologies. There is thus plenty of room for exploring the wonders of protein chemical topology with respect to their complexity and functional benefits.
## Chemical Tools Are Key to Expanding the Scope of Protein Topology
In synthetic polymers, varying chain topology has been routinely practiced to tune the property of polymers, as demonstrated in star polymers, cyclic polymers, and dendrimers. Translating this knowledge into proteins, however, is not straightforward. In DNA nanotechnology, the synthesis of topological structures is facilitated by highly programmable DNA assembly and the availability of various ligases [42]. Such chemical tools are less common for proteins. In chemical synthesis, tools such as ‘click’ chemistry are very useful for bioconjugation, but this approach may suffer from either a limited degree of polymerization or a lack of sequence control. Recombinant DNA technology allows the use of cellular machinery for the production of artificial proteins with designed sequence and length. In addition, the entire gene clusters responsible for the multi-step processing of precursor proteins into cyclotides or lasso peptides can be moved to E. coli to enable their heterologous biosynthesis [43,44]. The semi-synthetic
