approaches take advantage of a thioester-forming intein domain to facilitate subsequent ligation/cyclization, which is referred to as expressed protein ligation [45]. Such ligation can also be mediated by enzymes, such as subtiligase, sortase A, and butelase 1 [46,47]. Examples include synthetic cyclotides, cyclic proteins, and protein–polypeptide hybrids [48], and the peripheral functionalization of a polypeptide dendrimer [49]. Nonetheless, it is highly desired to take advantage of the intrinsic reactivity of natural amino acids in a specific and efficient way, eliminating the need for additional reagents.
The split intein technology offers the opportunity to do ligation in cells. The short peptide segment intein-C and its protein partner intein-N can reconstitute and splice in situ [50]. It has been used to achieve head-to-tail cyclization of various proteins in vivo, including maltose binding protein [51], lactamase [52], pseudostellarin F [53], GFP [54], and xylanase [55], among others. Similar to sortase-mediated ligation, it is incapable of generating branched architectures. In recent years, genetically encoded peptide–protein reactions or ‘molecular superglues’ $(\mathsf{B o x}3)$ ) have emerged as a unique way to do protein conjugation with intact N and C termini both in vitro and in vivo. This powerful toolbox is represented by the SpyTag/SpyCatcher chemistry [23]. Various topological proteins, such as cyclic elastin-like polypeptides (ELPs) [9], $\upbeta$ -lactamase and dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) [56], luciferase [57] and lichenase [58], and 3- arm/4-arm star proteins and H-shaped branched ELPs, have been successfully synthesized. Using folded protein as a template has yielded protein catenanes, mechanically interlocked tadpole proteins, and star proteins [10–12], contributing to greater topological diversity. However, it leaves a complex of undesirably large size $(>10\mathsf{k}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{a})$ . Efforts to reduce the size of the ligation scar are demonstrated by the development of SpyLigase [31] and SnoopLigase [32]. These tools have added tremendously to the chemical assets for topology manipulation. More recently, it was found that the catalytic triad could be artificially programmed into a globular protein for isopeptide formation [59]. Although the generality has yet to be proven, it promises a concise way to vary the protein chemical topology with minimum disturbance of the original structure.
Another intriguing chemical toolset is proximity-promoted intra- and inter-molecular reactions, enabled by the incorporation of reactive unnatural amino acids [60]. Its reactivity with thiols on the cysteine residues is fine-tuned to be inactive at low concentrations (or when they are far apart). Protein folding brings two reactive groups in close proximity to promote the reaction, producing a stable linkage and a stapled secondary structure with unusual backbone topology. Notably, this method is genetically programmable, and the product contains few ‘unnatural’ components. Hence, it has great potential in generating diverse protein topologies. Overall, chemical tools laid the basis of protein topology engineering.
## Artificial Proteins with Designed Topologies and Mechanical Bonds
The topological features of proteins are a result of natural evolution over millions of years. Even so, nature has just explored a mere fraction of all possible protein topologies [7]. To unleash the full potential of protein topology engineering, it is wise to start with mimicking what nature does in creating the topological features. The formidable protein complexity obscures a straightforward approach to their design and synthesis. While changing linear chains to rings, tadpoles, stars, and branched proteins can be as simple as one-step conjugation, it is difficult to design a nontrivial protein topology de novo. It requires precise control over not only protein folding but also post-translational modifications such as cleavage and ligation. Creative approaches are thus called for to achieve these complex topologies, especially those with mechanical bonds, toward the goal of ‘braiding’ protein chains on demand.
Engineering the relative positions of secondary structure motifs was once considered as topology engineering in protein science. Despite that the backbone remains linear, the spatial relationship is changed to gain certain functional benefits. By redesigning topology, Hilvert and coworkers changed the AroQ enzyme from homodimer to monomers and improved its activity via directed evolution [61]. ‘Protein rethreading’ was recently demonstrated in DHFR [62]. It
proposes that the secondary structure motifs of a folded protein can be rewired in space into a multi-swapped sequence, forming a well-folded structure resembling the original structure. By tandem repeat, Yeates and coworkers successfully designed an artificial knotted protein from an entwined dimer of HP0242 from Helicobacter pylori and unambiguously proved the knotted structure by crystallography [63]. A highly conserved, robust folded structure in 3D space is the foundation of these engineering strategies.
Complex topologies could be designed from simple building blocks. Natural topological proteins are an abundant resource of such building blocks. Yeates and coworkers engineered reactive cysteines into human carbonic anhydrase II to form either ‘serially knotted polymer’ or ‘superficially knotted polymer’ under oxidative conditions and carefully studied their effects on the improvement of thermal stability [64]. The interconversion between different topologies provides another effective approach. For example, Allen and Link reported the rational design of rotaxanes and catenanes from the lasso peptide precursor MccJ25 [44]. The backbone of MccJ25 was slightly modified to introduce two reactive cysteines and one lysine for trypsin cleavage. These mutations were tolerated during the lasso peptide processing. The trypsin digestion at the loop converts the lasso topology into a pseudorotaxane and subsequent intermolecular disulfide bond formation leads to [3]catenane and [4]catenane (Figure 2C). It elegantly demonstrates the robustness of the lasso motif and the possibility to generate complex topologies by directed topology interconversion.
An alternative way to create new topology while circumventing de novo design is to repurpose existing folding modules for topology diversification with the aid of bioconjugation, a strategy referred to as the ‘assembly-reaction’ synergy (Figure 2D) [65]. This concept was borrowed from supramolecular chemistry where it was developed to enable synthesis of highly complex molecular architectures such as the Star of David catenane [66] and the tightest molecular $8_{19}$ knot to date [67]. It is natural to envision that the entangling motifs could be used as templates to create topological proteins and mechanoproteins. For example, the p53tet domain in tumor suppressor p53 forms an intertwined homodimer with bisecting U monomers [68]. This entangling motif is so robust that a tandem repeat of p53tet domain can result in a compact knot-like structure [69]. Dawson and coworkers were the first to use the p53tet domain and its dimeric form p53dim to achieve protein catenation via solid-state phase synthesis and native chemical ligation [70,71]. Pseudorotaxanes and heterocatenanes were also prepared after some adaption [72].
The advent of genetically encoded peptide–protein chemistry (Box 3) makes this approach even more general. A gene cassette containing SpyTag-ELP-p53dim-ELP-SpyCatcher was designed so that p53dim would guide the entwining of two nascent chains in vivo and the SpyTag/SpyCatcher reaction could close the ring to give protein catenanes [10]. The strongly concatenating module SpyX further facilitates in vivo topology engineering to give star proteins [11]. Not only does changing the relative location of reactive sites and p53dim give different topologies but also the approach tolerates the inclusion of additional folded protein domains such as GB1, GFP, and DHFR [12]. In other words, the sequences and length can be tuned on the genetic level without disturbing the targeting protein topology in most cases. We envision that this strategy may be applied to other entangling motifs as well to generate even more complex topologies such as Borromean rings and trefoils.
## Functional Benefits of Complex Topologies
In nature, unusual topologies are evolved for functional benefits. The super-stabilities and robust bioactivities are characteristic of cyclotides and lasso peptides and are consistent with
their roles as chemical weapons in organisms. The lasso topology confines the conformation of the loop to exhibit optimal activity, whereas the tadpole counterpart could be totally inactive [73]. Similarly, the knotted topology increases the local conformational rigidity, thereby enhancing the tolerance toward mutation especially in places around the active sites [17]. The HK97 capsid exhibits extraordinary stability toward denaturants and heat for protecting the genetic information [19]. Despite this knowledge, the relationship between topology and biofunction has not yet been elucidated. We envision that artificial proteins could probably benefit from their topologies and/or mechanical bonds with regard to the following aspects (Figure 3).
First, topology can enhance stability and improve resilience. Generally, backbone cyclization reduces the chain entropy of the unfolded state and thus increases the stability of the folded state [74]. Catenation adds another stabilization effect: the chain interlocking that constrains the relative movement of chains [75]. Indeed, the p53dim catenane has a melting point of $89^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is $69^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ higher than its linear counterpart [71]. The effect also exists for large folded proteins. It was found that GFP catenane indeed exhibits a $C_{\mathrm{m}}~0.9~\mathsf{M}$ higher and DHFR catenane has a melting temperature $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ higher than the corresponding linear/cyclic counterparts [12]. Notably, the resistance to proteolytic digestion is significantly enhanced in protein catenanes, probably due to the clustering effect and the interlocking of protein subunits [10]. It suggests that topology engineering may be a convenient way to enhance the circulation time of therapeutic proteins in vivo, to prolong the life time of industrially relevant enzymes, or to stabilize proteins in a mechanical field. The enhanced stability also makes the protein more evolvable [76] (Figure 3A).
Second, mechanical bonds can bring in intriguing dynamic properties (Figure 3B). Examples prevail in supramolecular chemistry such as molecular machines and artificial muscles. Proteins often rely on conformational changes for allosteric regulation of enzymatic activity. Mechanical bond-based switches are rare in nature. Thermally actuated lasso peptide benenodin-1 is an excellent example [18]. Such motifs may be used in protein biomaterials in vitro or as dynamic switches in vivo, offering broad range of opportunities for functional manipulation.
Third, the multivalency effect can be exploited for synergistic functional enhancement (Figure 3C). High surface functionality and multivalency effect are common features of star polymers. This can also be useful for enhancing the binding affinity of ligands and improving the performance of enzymes and vaccines [77]. For example, a star polypeptide was found to possess highly potent antimicrobial activity for combating multi-drug resistance [78].
Finally, topology could facilitate the control of quaternary structures. Geyer and coworkers used SpyTag-SpyCatcher ligation chemistry to achieve modular assembly of antibody fragments [79]. It not only mimicked natural antibodies but also yielded configurations that were not present in nature. Using orthogonal reactions, Howarth and coworkers prepared thin lines of programmed polyproteins (polymers of protein subunits) and found a proper combination of protein sequences exhibiting the optimal effects for stimulating death factors [22]. Combined with avidin-biotin complexation, precise nanoassemblies containing SpyAvidin modules were prepared where the dodecomer exhibits the best T cell signaling performance [80]. In these cases, the quaternary structure is not only controlled by the sequence of the protein subunits, but also by the folded structures of the resulting covalent complex from ligation. The quaternary structure control, in synergy with the multivalency effect, is a remarkable feature of protein topology engineering.
With all these features, it is possible to construct highly stable, large complexes to emulate what nature does in making functional protein machineries such as proteasomes and ribosomes. Artificial protein machinery can be designed and prepared using mechanical bonds as robust and switchable links between spatially defined subunits for synergistic enhancement of protein activity. Hence, protein topology engineering presents a multitude of potential functional benefits that are accessible even without varying the native sequence of the protein of interest.
## Current Challenges for Protein Topology Engineering
Despite considerable progress, there are still limited examples of topological proteins, both natural and artificial. Protein topology engineering is full of challenges, and there are no simple solutions. Yet, its study will be highly rewarding. First, natural proteins are a vastly rich resource of topological proteins. Advanced gene mining techniques could be developed to enable efficient discovery of such proteins. These motifs are intriguing research objects by themselves and could be used to derive other topological proteins as well. Second, methods and techniques should be developed to facilitate the characterization of protein topology. The most unambiguous evidence for protein topology is crystallography, but it does not work for disordered proteins. The development of advanced NMR spectrometry and transmission electron cryomicroscopy provides valuable techniques in this aspect. Third, unique and powerful chemical tools should be developed to enable concise synthesis of complex protein topologies. To further extend the utility of assemblyreaction synergy, we need to identify more entangling motifs as templates and develop more ligationtoolsfor covalent fixation. It would be great ifthese motifsaresmall, effective,and efficient in directing the formation of topological proteins. Finally, it is necessary to demonstrate the utility of protein topology engineering in specific contexts for targeted applications (e.g., enzymology in protein engineering, artificial protein machinery in synthetic biology, mechanical parts in biomaterials). It takes a highly interdisciplinary approach to make strong cases for protein topology engineering. As the synthesis of topological proteins matures, the full potential of topology engineering in proteins will unfold.
## Concluding Remarks and Future Perspectives
The chemical topology of proteins is not dictated by the central dogma. It takes extensive posttranslational processing events to make these topological proteins in nature, as exemplified by cyclotides and lasso peptides. The genetically encoded peptide–protein chemistry makes it possible to program their biosynthesis, which leads to nonlinear proteins such as cyclic proteins, tadpole proteins, 3-arm or 4-arm star proteins, and H-shaped branched proteins. The assemblyreaction synergy further expands the scope of accessible protein topologies to include protein catenanes and mechanically interlocked star proteins. Such mechanoproteins are rare in nature. The presence of mechanical bonds adds to the complexity of protein architecture, making mechanical switching a possible mechanism for regulating enzyme activity and protein stability. With proper design, it may be possible to ‘braid’ or ‘weave’ protein chains into precisely defined topologies with desired properties in the future. We seek to understand better the relationship between chemical topology and the proteins’ physical and chemical properties (see Outstanding Questions). We feel that tools need to be continuously sharpened for preparing, characterizing, and exploiting these topological proteins. In his Nobel Prize lecture, Prof. Hermann Staudinger stated that ‘In the light of this new knowledge of macromolecular chemistry, the wonder of life in its chemical aspect is revealed in the astounding abundance and masterly macromolecular architecture of living matter.’ Similarly, we are fascinated by ‘the wonder of proteins in its topological aspect’ and are continuously amazed by ‘the astounding abundance and masterly complexity of proteinarchitectures’.Withfullcontroloverlength, sequence, stereochemistry,andtopology,they can be considered as the role model of precision macromolecules. The convergence of supramolecular chemistry, polymer chemistry, and protein engineering has paved this particular road to new horizons where complex topology is bound to endow proteins with emerging properties not seen in their components.
